19:07:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right to be skeptical. The phrase "IPv6 is the plan" has been repeated for over two decades, and for most of that time, it felt like a distant, optional upgrade with no teeth. But the situation has shifted significantly—not because of a global deadline or enforcement, but because **IPv4 exhaustion finally creates real, unavoidable pain.** Here’s why it’s not "no plan at all," even if it happened organically: ### 1. **No single authority can enforce IPv6** — the internet is decentralized The internet has no "IPv6 police." The IETF, RIRs, and ICANN can recommend, but they can’t force Comcast, Google, or your home router manufacturer to switch. So the "plan" was always technical standards + market pressure, not a government-style mandate. That’s why it took so long. ### 2. **IPv4 worked well enough for decades** — until it didn’t For years, NAT and conservation tricks (CIDR, private addresses) stretched IPv4 far beyond its original design. Most users never hit a wall. The incentive to deploy IPv6 was low: why spend money when IPv4-with-NAT still works? ### 3. **Now the pain is real** — and it’s forcing adoption IPv4 **is** exhausted. You can’t get a new block for free. The secondary market is active and prices are rising. This has pushed: - **Mobile carriers** (who need many IPs per subscriber) to adopt IPv6 heavily. In many countries, over 80% of mobile traffic is IPv6. - **Large ISPs in Asia and Europe** to deploy CGNAT and IPv6 dual-stack. In the US, Comcast, AT&T, and Verizon now have >70% IPv6 capability. - **Cloud providers** (AWS, Google, Azure) to support IPv6 natively because their growth depended on it. Worldwide, IPv6 adoption passed **40%** of all internet traffic in 2024, and it’s climbing fast. That’s not a mandate — it’s market forces. ### 4. **No deadline, but a de facto one** — IPv4 address prices If you need a /24 (256 IPs) for a new data center, it now costs ~$5,000–$10,000 on the transfer market. Compare that to a few hundred dollars a decade ago. That rising cost *is* the enforcement mechanism. Companies that drag their feet will pay more for legacy IPs, or get stuck behind worse NAT. ### 5. **So what about the "no real plan" feeling?** Your frustration is valid. The transition has been messy, slow, and full of half-measures. But: - **The plan *actually exists***: It’s called dual-stack (both IP versions), plus transition mechanisms like NAT64/DNS64 for IPv6-only networks. These are standardized and deployed. - **There have been deadlines**, but they’re soft: The US government mandated IPv6 for federal networks by 2008 (mostly failed), but later revisions made it a "must support" for procurement. Similar deadlines exist in other countries (China, India) but aren’t strictly enforced. ### Bottom line You’re right that a single, hard "IPv6 or else" date never came. But the combination of **IPv4 exhaustion, rising costs, and organic growth of IPv6-dependent services** (Apple devices, Google, Facebook, major CDNs) is now acting as a natural, gradual expiration date. We’re not flipping a switch — we’re replacing the old network one NAT at a time. And unlike 10 years ago, it’s finally happening at scale.
